use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Announcement import Announcement
from ../../../behaviors/Dragging import Dragging
from ../../buttons/IconButton import IconButton

export ui-component Carousel:
    description:
        A carousel shows a series of slides, such as photos or featured items,
        a page at a time in a strip the user moves along. Buttons move to the
        next and previous page, and a row of markers shows which page is
        current and lets the user jump to another.

    composes:
        - @{IconButton}

    behaviors:
        - @{Dragging}
        - @{Announcement}

    pages:
        A page holds one slide, or several side by side. Moving on moves a
        whole page, or a set number of slides. Slides can all be the same
        size or each a different width. The strip can run across or up and
        down, and one running across is reversed for right-to-left languages.
        The next slides can peek in at the edge, to show there is more.

    current:
        The component using the carousel is told when the page changes, and
        can move it to any page or slide.

    buttons:
        The next and previous buttons move one step. At the first and last
        page they are disabled, unless the carousel loops, in which case it
        wraps round to the other end.

    markers:
        There is one marker per page, and the current one is marked. Pressing
        a marker moves to its page. With a marker focused, the arrow keys move
        to the next and previous page, and Home and End to the first and last.

    swiping:
        On a touch screen the user swipes the strip along, and it settles on
        the nearest page. A trackpad or scroll wheel moves it too. The
        component decides whether it can also be dragged with a mouse.

    slides:
        Each slide tells assistive tools which one it is, such as "3 of 8".
        Slides out of view are hidden from assistive tools and can't be
        reached with the keyboard, so Tab never lands on something that can't
        be seen.

    announcing:
        When the user moves to another page, assistive tools are told about
        the slides now in view. While the carousel is moving by itself, this is
        not announced, so the user isn't interrupted every few seconds.

    autoplay:
        The component can have the carousel move on by itself at a set pace,
        looping round at the end. A button lets the user stop and start it.
        It pauses while the pointer is over the carousel and while the page
        can't be seen, and carries on afterwards. It stops for good when focus
        moves into the carousel or the user moves it along themselves, until
        they start it again.

    nonGoals:
        - Not for switching between panels of content with named headings.
            That's tabs
        - No endless strip that moves smoothly by itself. That's a Marquee
        - No thumbnails, zooming, or full-screen view. The app can add those
            around it
